we begin this morning with diplomacy  being put to the test as the u.s and its  nato allies try to diffuse the tension  with russia over its possible invasion  of ukraine secretary of state anthony  blinken met with his russian counterpart  sergey lavrov in geneva switzerland  friday blinken told lavrov the u.s would  give russia written responses to  moscow's proposals next week there is  some hope that an invasion of ukraine is  delayed at least for now even though  both sides appear to be far apart on  larger potentially more consequential  issues russia has deployed tens of  thousands of troops along its border  with ukraine and its neighboring belarus  christina raffini is in geneva with the  latest christina good morning  good morning dana those meetings here  yesterday lasted about 90 minutes both  sides called them frank and productive  and although there were no major  breakthroughs they agreed to keep  talking but this comes as allies are  rushing weapons to ukraine in case  diplomacy fails  we  have  made clear  and done  a number of things  in support and defense of ukraine  that  that will continue  but speaking to reporters in geneva  friday secretary blinken made clear that  support will not include u.s ground  forces  ukraine is not not a member of nato it's  not covered by the article 5 commitment  following a high profile meeting with  his russian counterpart blinken said the  u.s is doing everything it can to help  ukraine in other ways  we think that's the best and most  effective way  to  convince russia not to  engage in further aggression against  ukraine overnight 200 thousand pounds of  lethal aid including ammunition arrived  in kiev  and the u.s has also given permission  for other nato allies to rush u.s made  weapons to ukraine's eastern front it's  good to see you as well then there's the  diplomatic front  what we expect is concrete answers to  our concrete proposals the bottom line  is this this is a critical moment  russia says it has no plans to invade  and its troop buildup is defensive but  the u.s has questioned how a country  with such a robust military could feel  threatened by its much smaller neighbor  is russia intimidated by ukraine why are  you afraid of over ukraine why do you  feel you need to protect yourself as we  are not afraid of anyone even not of the  us you know  secretary blinken said after consulting  with president biden he should be able  to have that written response to  moscow's proposal something russia has  been asking for by next week michelle  frank question christina glad you were  able to ask it  tomorrow on face the nation margaret  brennan speaks with secretary of state  anthony blinken
 

